    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I see what you mean about their traits. But it feels weird that for Kenji, being \"brilliant\" and \"disciplined\" makes him fit into a prestige/high-society vibe, whereas for Jax and Andre, being \"smart\" or \"knowledgeable\" about their surroundings automatically pushes them into the crime genre. \n\nIf I swapped their backgrounds—like if Kenji was the one living a very disciplined life on a street corner, or if Jax was a high-end engineer—would that change how \"natural\" they feel in those genres? Or do you think certain personality types just inherently gravitate toward one of those social tiers regardless of their race? I'm worried I've accidentally made it so that \"intelligence/order\" = high society and \"street smarts/community knowledge\" = crime drama.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You've hit on a very common, and frankly, lazy writing shortcut. Yes, the way you've framed it leans heavily on those tired tropes, associating high intellect with polished wealth and street knowledge with lower-class struggle. If you swap them, the *genre* might change, but the *tension* you're aiming for will be much stronger. A brilliant engineer on the street is inherently more interesting than a suited businessman, and a street-smart engineer in a penthouse creates immediate conflict. Don't let the labels dictate the character; let the conflict between their traits and their environment drive the story.",
      "source": "target",
